30,2,,status,Benjamin,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":1,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
31,2,,status,Mizuki,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
32,2,,status,Toshio,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":3,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
33,2,,status,Victoria,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
34,2,,status,George,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
35,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"My findings suggest that projects benefiting a wide user base are far more successful. Conversely, older blocks hide risks that cause significant delays.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Victoria,,I found that we consistently fail to plan for temporary space. The cost of hiring off-site rooms is a hidden drain on our budget.,"{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,George,,The data is concerning. Our bench technicians are wasting hours on manual routines that could be automated with the right facility upgrades.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Mizuki,,"My data confirms a systemic issue. We are ignoring weather risks and over-promising on ""prestige"" projects, leading to inevitable schedule collapses.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,How delightful. It seems we are all discovering that our planning is a work of fiction. The tender records show costs rising far beyond our estimates.,"{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Benjamin, if costs are rising, we must prioritize projects with the highest utility. We cannot afford a niche project that fails to satisfy users.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Victoria,,"Exactly. And we must consider the ""cost"" of the room being out of service. A project that disrupts the timetable too much is too expensive.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,George,,Utility is not just about the number of users. It is about the sustainability of the work. We are burning out our technicians.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@George, I sympathize, but we must be careful. If we choose a complex upgrade that is prone to delays, we only worsen the staff's burden.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"Precisely. Furthermore, we have a habit of forgetting that equipment dies. We have no reserves for AV replacement, making ""modern"" plans a liability.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"It seems we have a conflict between specialist needs and general utility. I believe the general-use space is the safer, more effective investment.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Victoria,,I agree. The post-occupancy ratings were clear: general-use spaces provide the most value and are less likely to cause localized timetable crises.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,George,,I cannot support a general-use project if it ignores the operational crisis. We need a plan that reduces the manual labor for our staff.,"{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Mizuki,,"We must weigh the risk of delay. A project that is ""essential"" for staff but complex to build might slip and leave us with nothing for two years.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,Let us be rational. A project with volatile market costs or hidden maintenance debts is a trap. We should favor the most predictable budget.,"{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
