60,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"Option B, the Study Commons, aligns best with the data. It serves the widest user base and avoids the high-risk zones of the older blocks.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Victoria,,"I support Option B. It is a conversion of an underused hall, meaning it won't disrupt the active timetable or require expensive off-site room hire.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,George,,I must advocate for Option A. The ventilation upgrade directly addresses the manual routines that are exhausting our technicians. It is a matter of staff welfare.,"{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Mizuki,,"Option A is a high-risk project. Ventilation work in old buildings often reveals unforeseen site conditions, which leads to the exact delays I fear.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"Option D is a financial fantasy. Given the lack of renewal reserves for AV kit, we would be building a center that becomes obsolete in five years.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"@George, while I acknowledge the staff's pain, the post-occupancy data shows that specialized upgrades often disappoint if they don't benefit the broader community.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Victoria,,"@George, if we choose Option A, how do we handle the loss of the teaching labs during the work? The timetable cannot absorb that loss.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,George,,"We have survived disruptions before, but we cannot survive the loss of our staff. Option A is the only one that provides actual relief to the workers.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@George, the ""surviving"" you mention is exactly the problem. We squeeze a year of building into a few weeks. Option B is far more predictable.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"Option C is safe, but perhaps too boring. However, Option B is the most fiscally responsible, as it avoids the volatile mechanical plant costs of Option A.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Toshio,,Option B is the most rational choice. It maximizes user satisfaction and minimizes the risk of failure. It is the most reliable path forward.,"{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Victoria,,I agree. It avoids the hidden costs of temporary space and provides a high-rating outcome for the student body. I vote for Option B.,"{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,George,,"I cannot in good conscience vote for a ""study space"" while my colleagues are manually opening windows because the vents are broken. I stand by Option A.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Mizuki,,"I cannot support Option A or D. The risk of scheduling slips is too high. Option B is a low-risk, high-reward project. I vote for Option B.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"While I appreciate the nobility of Option A, the market for mechanical plant is currently a nightmare. Option B is the only one that won't bankrupt us.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
